= Glossary of Medical and Scientific Terms
<app:glossary>

This glossary defines medical, biochemical, immunological, and statistical terms used throughout this document. Terms are organized alphabetically. Where a term is used in a specialized sense specific to ME/CFS research, the ME/CFS-specific usage is indicated.


== A

/ *4-PBA (4-phenylbutyrate)*: A sodium phenylbutyrate chemical chaperone that reduces endoplasmic reticulum stress by facilitating protein folding and attenuating the unfolded protein response.
/ *Acetyl-CoA*: Acetyl coenzyme A; the two-carbon unit that enters the Krebs cycle, produced from pyruvate (via pyruvate dehydrogenase), fatty acids (via $beta$-oxidation), or amino acids.
/ *ACTH*: Adrenocorticotropic hormone; pituitary hormone that stimulates cortisol production by the adrenal cortex. Part of the HPA axis.
/ *Adenosine triphosphate (ATP)*: The primary intracellular energy currency. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP releases energy for cellular processes.
/ *Aerobic glycolysis*: Glycolysis occurring in the presence of oxygen, with pyruvate entering the Krebs cycle rather than being converted to lactate. Distinct from anaerobic glycolysis.
/ *ALA (Alpha-lipoic acid)*: A mitochondrial cofactor and potent antioxidant that regenerates other antioxidants (glutathione, vitamins C and E) and supports mitochondrial energy metabolism.
/ *Allostatic load*: The cumulative physiological cost of chronic stress and adaptation. In ME/CFS, elevated allostatic load reflects multi-system dysregulation.
/ *AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK)*: A cellular energy sensor activated by high AMP/ATP ratios. Triggers catabolic pathways to restore energy balance.
/ *Anaerobic threshold (AT)*: The exercise intensity above which lactate production exceeds clearance, causing blood lactate to rise. Reduced in ME/CFS.
/ *Antihistamines*: A class of drugs that block histamine receptors. H1 antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine) target allergic and mast-cell-mediated symptoms; H2 antihistamines (famotidine, cimetidine) block gastric H2 receptors with additional immunomodulatory effects.
/ *Autoantibody*: An antibody directed against the body's own proteins. Autoantibodies against adrenergic and muscarinic receptors have been identified in ME/CFS subsets.
/ *Autonomic nervous system (ANS)*: The division of the nervous system controlling involuntary functions (heart rate, blood pressure, digestion) through sympathetic and parasympathetic branches.

== B

/ *Baroreflex*: The reflex arc that maintains blood pressure by adjusting heart rate and vascular tone in response to changes detected by arterial baroreceptors.
/ *Bell Disability Scale*: A 0–100 rating scale for functional capacity in ME/CFS, where 100 represents normal function and 0 represents bedridden and dependent.
/ *Beta-blockers*: Drugs that antagonize beta-adrenergic receptors. Used in ME/CFS for POTS and adrenergic symptoms; may also block $beta$2-adrenergic autoantibodies (speculative).
/ *Beta2-adrenergic autoantibodies ($beta$2-Abs)*: Autoantibodies targeting the $beta$2-adrenergic receptor, identified in ME/CFS subsets. May contribute to vascular and autonomic dysfunction.
/ *Bistability*: A dynamical system property in which two stable steady states coexist for the same parameter values. Transition between states requires a perturbation exceeding a threshold.
/ *Blood–brain barrier (BBB)*: The selective permeability barrier formed by endothelial cells of cerebral capillaries, restricting passage of molecules and immune cells into the CNS.
/ *Butyrate*: A short-chain fatty acid produced by gut bacteria from dietary fiber. Maintains intestinal barrier integrity and exerts anti-inflammatory effects.

== C

/ *Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET)*: A diagnostic test measuring oxygen consumption, carbon dioxide production, and ventilation during graded exercise. The two-day CPET protocol is used to objectively demonstrate PEM.
/ *Cell danger response (CDR)*: A conserved metabolic response to cellular threat, characterized by reduced mitochondrial function and extracellular ATP/ADP signaling. Proposed as a unifying mechanism in ME/CFS by Naviaux.
/ *Central sensitization*: Enhanced responsiveness of central nervous system neurons to input, resulting in amplified pain and sensory perception.
/ *Cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway*: A neuroimmune reflex in which vagal efferent signals suppress macrophage TNF-$alpha$ production through $alpha$7 nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.
/ *Cimetidine*: An H2 receptor antagonist with additional immunomodulatory properties through T-cell modulation and enhanced NK cell activity. Investigated as an adjunctive immunomodulator in ME/CFS.
/ *Circadian rhythm*: Endogenous biological oscillations with approximately 24-hour periodicity, governed by the suprachiasmatic nucleus and peripheral clock genes.
/ *Coenzyme Q10 (CoQ10)*: Ubiquinone; an electron carrier in the mitochondrial electron transport chain between Complexes I/II and Complex III. Also functions as a lipid-soluble antioxidant.
/ *Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH)*: Hypothalamic hormone that initiates the HPA axis cascade by stimulating pituitary ACTH release.
/ *Cortisol*: The primary glucocorticoid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. Regulates metabolism, immune function, and stress response. Mildly reduced in ME/CFS.
/ *Creatine*: A nitrogenous compound involved in ATP regeneration through the phosphocreatine system. Supplementation may improve muscle energy metabolism and cognitive function.
/ *Cytokine*: A small signaling protein secreted by immune cells that mediates and regulates immunity, inflammation, and hematopoiesis. Key examples: IL-1$beta$, IL-6, TNF-$alpha$, IFN-$gamma$, IL-10.

== D–E

/ *D-Ribose*: A pentose sugar that serves as the backbone of ATP. Supplementation is hypothesized to support ATP synthesis under conditions of impaired energy metabolism.
/ *DAO (Diamine oxidase)*: The primary enzyme responsible for histamine degradation in the gut. Reduced DAO activity contributes to histamine intolerance, which overlaps with MCAS symptoms.
/ *DePaul Symptom Questionnaire (DSQ)*: A validated self-report instrument for assessing ME/CFS symptom frequency and severity across multiple domains.
/ *Digital twin*: A patient-specific computational model continuously updated with real-time data to predict individual disease trajectory and treatment response.
/ *DORA (dual orexin receptor antagonist)*: A class of sleep medications (suvorexant, lemborexant, daridorexant) that block both OX1R and OX2R orexin receptors. Promotes sleep by reducing orexin-mediated wakefulness. May have neuroprotective effects via PKA pathway inhibition independent of sleep promotion.
/ *Dornase alfa*: Recombinant human DNase I that cleaves extracellular DNA; used to degrade neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) in conditions involving excessive NETosis.
/ *Droxidopa*: A synthetic norepinephrine prodrug that crosses the blood–brain barrier and is converted to norepinephrine both centrally and peripherally by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase. Used for neurogenic orthostatic hypotension.
/ *Duloxetine*: A serotonin–norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) used for neuropathic pain, fibromyalgia, and comorbid depression in ME/CFS.
/ *Dysautonomia*: Dysfunction of the autonomic nervous system. In ME/CFS, manifests as orthostatic intolerance, POTS, temperature dysregulation, and gastrointestinal dysmotility.
/ *Electron transport chain (ETC)*: The series of protein complexes (I–IV) in the inner mitochondrial membrane that transfer electrons from NADH/FADH#sub[2] to oxygen, generating the proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis.
/ *Energy envelope*: The maximum amount of energy a patient can expend without triggering post-exertional malaise. Concept formalized by Jason et al.
/ *Epigenetics*: Heritable changes in gene expression that do not involve changes to the DNA sequence. Includes DNA methylation, histone modification, and non-coding RNA regulation. Epigenetic alterations have been documented in ME/CFS.
/ *ER stress*: Endoplasmic reticulum stress; the accumulation of misfolded proteins in the ER that activates the unfolded protein response (UPR). Implicated in ME/CFS as a contributor to cellular dysfunction and inflammation.

== F–G

/ *Fibromyalgia (FM)*: A chronic pain condition frequently comorbid with ME/CFS, characterized by widespread musculoskeletal pain and tenderness.
/ *Fludrocortisone*: A synthetic mineralocorticoid that expands plasma volume by promoting renal sodium retention. Used off-label for orthostatic intolerance and POTS in ME/CFS.
/ *Fluvoxamine*: A selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) with sigma-1 receptor (S1R) agonist activity. S1R agonism may reduce ER stress via chaperone-mediated UPR modulation.
/ *Fukuda criteria*: A 1994 case definition for CFS requiring $gt.eq 6$ months of unexplained fatigue plus $gt.eq 4$ of 8 specified symptoms. Less specific than the ICC or CCC criteria.
/ *Gabapentinoids*: A class of drugs (gabapentin, pregabalin) that bind the $alpha$2$delta$ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing neurotransmitter release. Used for neuropathic pain and sleep disturbance in ME/CFS.
/ *Ginkgo (Ginkgo biloba)*: A herbal extract with vasodilatory and antiplatelet properties. Hypothesized to improve cerebral perfusion and cognitive symptoms in ME/CFS.
/ *Glycyrrhizin*: A triterpenoid saponin from licorice root that inhibits HMGB1 release and binds directly to HMGB1. Proposed as an anti-DAMP therapy to interrupt sterile inflammation driven by extracellular HMGB1.
/ *Glymphatic system*: The brain's waste clearance system, dependent on aquaporin-4 water channels and active primarily during sleep. Impaired glymphatic function may contribute to unrefreshing sleep and cognitive dysfunction.
/ *Graded exercise therapy (GET)*: A structured exercise program involving progressive increases in physical activity. Contraindicated in ME/CFS per NICE 2021 guidelines due to risk of harm.
/ *Gut–brain axis*: The bidirectional communication system between the gastrointestinal tract and the central nervous system, involving neural (vagal), immune, endocrine, and microbial signaling pathways.

== H–I

/ *H1 receptor*: The histamine receptor type 1, mediating allergic and inflammatory responses. H1 antihistamines are first-line for mast cell activation symptoms.
/ *H2 receptor*: The histamine receptor type 2, primarily mediating gastric acid secretion. H2 antihistamines also modulate T-cell responses and have been investigated for immunomodulation in ME/CFS.
/ *Heart rate variability (HRV)*: The variation in time intervals between consecutive heartbeats. Reduced HRV indicates autonomic dysfunction, particularly reduced parasympathetic tone. Consistently abnormal in ME/CFS.
/ *Hill function*: A mathematical function describing cooperative binding or sigmoidal dose–response relationships: $f(x) = x^n / (K^n + x^n)$, where $n$ is the Hill coefficient and $K$ is the half-saturation constant.
/ *HMGB1 (High mobility group box 1)*: A nuclear protein that, when released extracellularly, functions as a damage-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) activating TLR4 and RAGE. Elevated extracellular HMGB1 is a proposed driver of sterile inflammation in ME/CFS.
/ *HPA axis*: Hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal axis; the neuroendocrine cascade (CRH $\to$ ACTH $\to$ cortisol) that regulates the stress response and cortisol production.
/ *Hypometabolism*: A state of reduced metabolic activity. In ME/CFS, refers to the systemic downregulation of metabolic pathways documented by metabolomics studies.
/ *IDO (Indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase)*: An enzyme that converts tryptophan to kynurenine. Upregulated by IFN-$gamma$, diverting tryptophan away from serotonin synthesis.
/ *IL-2 (Interleukin-2)*: A cytokine essential for T-cell proliferation and regulatory T-cell (Treg) function. Low-dose IL-2 therapy is hypothesized to expand Treg populations and restore immune tolerance.
/ *Immune exhaustion*: Progressive loss of effector function in T cells or NK cells due to chronic antigen stimulation. Characterized by upregulation of inhibitory receptors (PD-1, Tim-3, LAG-3).
/ *International Consensus Criteria (ICC)*: A 2011 case definition for ME requiring post-exertional neuroimmune exhaustion plus neurological, immune, and energy metabolism symptoms. More specific than the Fukuda criteria.
/ *Ivabradine*: A selective funny channel (If) inhibitor that reduces heart rate without affecting blood pressure or myocardial contractility. Used off-label for POTS with prominent tachycardia.
/ *IVIG (Intravenous immunoglobulin)*: Pooled human immunoglobulin G from thousands of donors, administered intravenously. Used off-label in ME/CFS to neutralize autoantibodies and modulate immune function.

== K–M

/ *Krebs cycle*: (Also: citric acid cycle, tricarboxylic acid cycle.) The metabolic pathway in the mitochondrial matrix that oxidizes acetyl-CoA to CO#sub[2], generating NADH, FADH#sub[2], and GTP.
/ *Kynurenine pathway*: The metabolic pathway converting tryptophan to nicotinamide (NAD#super[+] precursor) via kynurenine, 3-hydroxykynurenine, and quinolinic acid. Produces both neuroprotective and neurotoxic metabolites.
/ *L-Carnitine*: A quaternary ammonium compound that transports long-chain fatty acids into the mitochondrial matrix for $beta$-oxidation. Supplementation may improve energy production in mitochondrial dysfunction.
/ *LDA (Low-dose aripiprazole)*: Aripiprazole used at sub-antipsychotic doses (~0.25–2 mg), acting as a partial dopamine D2 agonist. Hypothesized to modulate microglial activation and dopamine tone in ME/CFS.
/ *Lipopolysaccharide (LPS)*: A component of gram-negative bacterial cell walls. A potent activator of innate immunity through TLR4 signaling. Elevated circulating LPS (endotoxemia) may result from intestinal barrier dysfunction in ME/CFS.
/ *Low-dose naltrexone (LDN)*: An opioid receptor antagonist used at sub-therapeutic doses (1–5mg) for immunomodulatory effects. Investigated as an off-label treatment for ME/CFS.
/ *Luteolin*: A flavonoid with mast-cell-stabilizing, antioxidant, and neuroprotective properties. Structural analog of quercetin; often used in combination mast-cell-stabilizing formulations.
/ *Magnesium*: An essential divalent cation required for >300 enzymatic reactions including ATP-dependent processes. Deficiency may exacerbate fatigue, muscle pain, sleep disturbance, and autonomic dysfunction.
/ *Mast cell activation syndrome (MCAS)*: A condition characterized by excessive mast cell mediator release, producing symptoms overlapping with ME/CFS (fatigue, brain fog, flushing, gastrointestinal disturbance).
/ *Melatonin*: A pineal hormone regulating circadian sleep–wake cycles. Used as a sleep aid with additional antioxidant and mitochondrial-protective properties.
/ *Metabolic trap*: A hypothesis proposing that ME/CFS involves a stable biochemical state in which a metabolic pathway remains locked in a dysfunctional configuration due to bistability in enzyme kinetics.
/ *Methyl-donor*: A nutrient that supplies methyl groups ($−"CH"#sub[3]$) for methylation reactions, including DNA methylation, neurotransmitter synthesis, and creatine synthesis. Key methyl-donors include methylfolate, methylcobalamin (B12), and S-adenosylmethionine (SAMe).
/ *Methylphenidate*: A dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor; a CNS stimulant used off-label for cognitive dysfunction and fatigue in ME/CFS.
/ *Michaelis–Menten kinetics*: A model of enzyme-catalyzed reaction rates: $v = V_("max")[S]/(K_m + [S])$, where $V_("max")$ is the maximal rate and $K_m$ is the substrate concentration at half-maximal rate.
/ *Microglia*: The resident immune cells of the central nervous system. Activated microglia produce neuroinflammatory mediators implicated in cognitive dysfunction and central sensitization.
/ *Midodrine*: An alpha-1 adrenergic receptor agonist that causes vasoconstriction and increases blood pressure. Used for orthostatic hypotension and POTS.
/ *Mitochondria*: Double-membrane organelles responsible for aerobic ATP production via the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain. Mitochondrial dysfunction is a central feature of ME/CFS pathophysiology.
/ *Modafinil*: A wakefulness-promoting agent that inhibits dopamine reuptake. Used off-label for fatigue and cognitive dysfunction in ME/CFS.

== N–O

/ *NAC (N-Acetylcysteine)*: A glutathione precursor and mucolytic with antioxidant properties. NAC replenishes intracellular glutathione and may reduce oxidative stress in ME/CFS.
/ *NAD#super[+]/NADH*: Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (oxidized/reduced forms). Essential electron carriers in cellular metabolism. The NAD#super[+]/NADH ratio reflects cellular redox state and regulates metabolic flux.
/ *Narcolepsy Type 1 (NT1)*: An autoimmune condition caused by T-cell-mediated destruction of $gt.eq$90% of hypothalamic orexin neurons, producing CSF orexin-A $lt$110 pg/mL, cataplexy, and excessive daytime sleepiness. Triggered by post-infectious molecular mimicry (H1N1, _Streptococcus_), strongly associated with HLA-DQB1\*06:02.
/ *Narcolepsy Type 2 (NT2)*: Narcolepsy without cataplexy, with intermediate CSF orexin-A levels (110--200 pg/mL "gray zone"). ME/CFS may overlap with NT2 through functional orexin suppression rather than autoimmune destruction (Section @spec:mecfs-functional-narcolepsy).
/ *Natural killer (NK) cells*: Innate immune lymphocytes that kill virally infected and transformed cells. Reduced NK cell cytotoxicity is one of the most replicated immunological findings in ME/CFS.
/ *NETosis*: A form of programmed neutrophil cell death in which neutrophils extrude extracellular traps (NETs) composed of DNA, histones, and antimicrobial proteins. Dysregulated NETosis contributes to thrombosis, autoimmunity, and sterile inflammation.
/ *Neuroinflammation*: Inflammation within the central nervous system, mediated primarily by activated microglia and astrocytes. PET imaging has demonstrated neuroinflammation in ME/CFS patients.
/ *NSAIDs (Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs)*: Drugs (ibuprofen, naproxen, celecoxib) that inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, reducing prostaglandin synthesis. Used for pain and inflammation but limited by gastrointestinal and renal toxicity.
/ *Omega-3 fatty acids*: Polyunsaturated fatty acids (EPA and DHA) that serve as precursors to specialized pro-resolving mediators (SPMs). May promote inflammation resolution rather than immunosuppression.
/ *Orexin (hypocretin)*: A pair of hypothalamic neuropeptides (orexin-A and orexin-B, also called hypocretin-1 and hypocretin-2) produced by ~70,000 neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Orexin regulates sleep-wake transitions, arousal, autonomic function, and energy homeostasis. Reduced orexin-A levels have been documented in ME/CFS and post-viral fatigue states. Loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy.
/ *Orthostatic intolerance (OI)*: Inability to tolerate upright posture, with symptoms (lightheadedness, palpitations, nausea, pre-syncope) that improve with recumbency. Affects the majority of ME/CFS patients.
/ *OX2R agonist*: A selective agonist of the orexin-2 receptor (OX2R). Danavorexton (TAK-925, IV) and oveporexton (TAK-861, oral) are in clinical development for narcolepsy. Hypothesized as potential ME/CFS treatments if functional orexin deficiency is confirmed.
/ *Oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS)*: The process by which ATP is synthesized using energy from the mitochondrial proton gradient generated by the electron transport chain.
/ *Oxidative stress*: An imbalance between reactive oxygen species production and antioxidant defense, resulting in cellular damage to lipids, proteins, and DNA.
/ *Oxycodone*: A semi-synthetic opioid analgesic acting at mu-opioid receptors. Used for severe pain refractory to other treatments; limited by tolerance and dependence.

== P–R

/ *Pacing*: An activity management strategy in which patients maintain energy expenditure within their energy envelope to prevent post-exertional malaise.
/ *PEA (Palmitoylethanolamide)*: An endogenous fatty acid amide that activates PPAR-$alpha$ and indirectly modulates the endocannabinoid system. Exerts anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and mast-cell-stabilizing effects via downregulation of microglial and mast cell activation.
/ *Pitolisant*: An H3 receptor antagonist/inverse agonist that increases histaminergic signaling in the brain. Approved for narcolepsy; investigated for cognitive dysfunction mediated by histaminergic deficits.
/ *Post-exertional malaise (PEM)*: The hallmark symptom of ME/CFS: a disproportionate worsening of symptoms following physical, cognitive, or emotional exertion, typically delayed by 12–72 hours and lasting days to weeks.
/ *Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS)*: A form of orthostatic intolerance defined by a sustained heart rate increase $gt.eq 30$bpm within 10 minutes of standing, without orthostatic hypotension.
/ *Pregnenolone*: A neurosteroid and precursor to all steroid hormones. Modulates ion channels including TRPM3; may partially compensate for TRPM3 dysfunction in ME/CFS by sustaining channel activity.
/ *Proton-motive force*: The electrochemical gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane, composed of the membrane potential ($Delta Psi$) and the pH gradient ($Delta$pH). Drives ATP synthesis by Complex V.
/ *Pyridostigmine*: An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that increases synaptic acetylcholine availability, enhancing parasympathetic tone. Used off-label for POTS and orthostatic intolerance in ME/CFS.
/ *Quercetin*: A flavonoid mast cell stabilizer that inhibits histamine release and leukotriene synthesis. Also possesses antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties.
/ *Reactive oxygen species (ROS)*: Chemically reactive molecules derived from molecular oxygen, including superoxide ($"O"_2^(dot.op-)$), hydrogen peroxide (H#sub[2]O#sub[2]), and hydroxyl radical ($dot.op$OH). Generated as byproducts of mitochondrial electron transport.
/ *Rituximab*: A monoclonal antibody targeting CD20 on B cells, causing B cell depletion. Investigated for ME/CFS based on an autoimmune hypothesis; phase III trial did not demonstrate efficacy.
/ *Rupatadine*: A dual H1 receptor antagonist and platelet-activating factor (PAF) antagonist; a second-generation antihistamine with additional anti-inflammatory activity through PAF inhibition.

== S–T

/ *S1R (Sigma-1 receptor)*: An endoplasmic reticulum chaperone protein that modulates ion channel function, Ca²⁺ signaling, and the unfolded protein response. S1R agonists like fluvoxamine may reduce ER stress.
/ *Sensitivity analysis*: A mathematical technique for determining which model parameters most strongly influence model outputs. Global sensitivity analysis (Sobol indices) partitions output variance across parameters and interactions.
/ *Separatrix*: In dynamical systems theory, the boundary between basins of attraction. In the ME/CFS bistability model, the separatrix divides state space into regions that converge to the healthy state versus the disease state.
/ *SF-36 (Short Form Health Survey)*: A 36-item questionnaire measuring eight domains of health-related quality of life. ME/CFS patients score lower than patients with most other chronic conditions.
/ *Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs)*: Fatty acids with fewer than 6 carbon atoms (acetate, propionate, butyrate) produced by bacterial fermentation of dietary fiber in the colon.
/ *Sickness behavior*: A coordinated set of behavioral changes (fatigue, social withdrawal, anorexia, hyperalgesia) triggered by pro-inflammatory cytokines acting on the brain. Proposed as a model for ME/CFS symptomatology.
/ *Sodium*: An essential electrolyte critical for plasma volume maintenance, nerve conduction, and muscle function. Increased sodium intake is recommended for POTS and orthostatic intolerance to support intravascular volume.
/ *Solriamfetol*: A dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor; a wakefulness-promoting agent approved for excessive daytime sleepiness in narcolepsy and obstructive sleep apnea. Investigated for ME/CFS-related fatigue.
/ *SPM (Specialized pro-resolving mediators)*: Lipid mediators (resolvins, protectins, maresins, lipoxins) derived from omega-3 fatty acids that actively terminate inflammation and promote tissue repair without immunosuppression.
/ *Sympathetic nervous system (SNS)*: The division of the autonomic nervous system responsible for “fight-or-flight” responses: increased heart rate, blood pressure, bronchodilation, and metabolic rate.
/ *Tilt table testing*: A diagnostic procedure for orthostatic intolerance in which the patient is passively tilted from supine to upright while heart rate and blood pressure are monitored.
/ *Trazodone*: An antidepressant with potent sedative properties via H1 receptor antagonism and alpha-1 blockade. Used off-label at low doses for sleep initiation in ME/CFS, without anticholinergic burden.
/ *TRPM3*: A non-selective cation channel of the transient receptor potential family, expressed on sensory neurons and immune cells. TRPM3 dysfunction, documented in ME/CFS natural killer cells, may impair calcium influx and immune surveillance.
/ *Tryptophan*: An essential amino acid and precursor for serotonin synthesis (via tryptophan hydroxylase) and kynurenine production (via IDO). Competition between these pathways is relevant to ME/CFS.
/ *TUDCA (Tauroursodeoxycholic acid)*: A bile acid derivative with chemical chaperone activity that reduces ER stress by stabilizing protein folding and inhibiting the unfolded protein response.
/ *tVNS (Transcutaneous vagus nerve stimulation)*: A non-invasive technique delivering electrical stimulation to the auricular branch of the vagus nerve. Proposed to enhance parasympathetic tone and activate the cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway.

== U–Z

/ *Ubiquinone*: See Coenzyme Q10.
/ *UPR (Unfolded protein response)*: A cellular stress response triggered by accumulation of misfolded proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum. The UPR initially restores proteostasis but, if unresolved, triggers apoptosis. Chronic UPR activation is implicated in ME/CFS, particularly through PERK and IRE1$alpha$ branches.
/ *Vagus nerve*: The tenth cranial nerve; the primary parasympathetic nerve providing afferent and efferent innervation to thoracic and abdominal organs. Mediates the cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway.
/ *Valacyclovir*: An oral prodrug of acyclovir with improved bioavailability; used to suppress herpesvirus (EBV, HHV-6) reactivation in subsets of ME/CFS patients with evidence of chronic viral infection.
/ *Valganciclovir*: An oral prodrug of ganciclovir with activity against cytomegalovirus (CMV) and HHV-6. Used off-label for herpesvirus reactivation in ME/CFS; requires monitoring for myelosuppression.
/ *Warburg effect*: The metabolic shift from oxidative phosphorylation to aerobic glycolysis, originally described in cancer cells but also characteristic of activated immune cells.
/ *Zolpidem*: A non-benzodiazepine hypnotic acting as a GABA-A receptor positive allosteric modulator at the $alpha$1 subunit. Used for sleep initiation in ME/CFS; may worsen sleep architecture quality despite reducing sleep latency.
